  <!-- 3. 24-48H DELTA -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive &middot; banned-verb lint enforced &middot; each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>US forces continued targeting IRGC air defense sites, radar installations, and maritime assets in southern Iran through early September, with CENTCOM also striking Iranian oil tankers following Iran&rsquo;s renewed attacks on commercial shipping after the June memorandum of understanding collapsed in July. The September strikes represented a tactical shift back toward direct military action after a period emphasizing economic-sanctions pressure. US aircraft carriers and strike packages in the Arabian Sea remained the primary delivery platform, with exchanges involving Iranian retaliation against US-linked bases in Jordan. The sustained kinetic tempo entering UNGA week established the operational backdrop against which the September 22 diplomatic contact occurred. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>On September 22, at the margins of the UN General Assembly in New York, US special envoys Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner engaged with an Iranian delegation through mediators who, in Witkoff&rsquo;s own words, &ldquo;shuttled between the two sides throughout the day.&rdquo; Iranian FM Abbas Araghchi separately confirmed to Iranian state broadcaster IRIB that he held a meeting with Witkoff &mdash; the first confirmed face-to-face contact between a named Iranian FM and a named US senior envoy since the Islamabad Talks in April. Witkoff described the session as &ldquo;promising and constructive&rdquo; and said another meeting was expected &ldquo;soon.&rdquo; Trump told reporters it was &ldquo;a very good, very productive meeting&rdquo; and described himself as facing &ldquo;a big decision&rdquo; between a deal and what he called &ldquo;annihilation.&rdquo; → moved <span class="cite">F8</span>, <span class="cite">F6</span></p>

      <p>Iran&rsquo;s delegation at UNGA delivered its operative Hormuz conditions to the US side: an immediate lifting of the US naval blockade of Iranian ports, the release of frozen Iranian assets, and an end to fighting on all fronts including Lebanon. A senior Iranian official told Al Jazeera that Tehran could reopen the strait within seven days once these conditions were met. The proposal had been submitted in writing through mediators on September 16, giving the US six days before the UNGA meeting to review it. Qatar announced it was working alongside Pakistan to safeguard freedom of navigation through the strait. As of EOD September 23, the US had not publicly accepted any component of Iran&rsquo;s conditions. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span>, <span class="cite">F9</span></p>

      <p>AIS-tracked commercial vessel transits through the Strait of Hormuz continued at historically depressed levels, falling to 7 transits on September 9 and partially recovering to 14&ndash;17 vessels over the September 20&ndash;21 weekend, against a pre-war baseline of approximately 125 large commercial vessels per day. No P&amp;I insurer has reinstated war-risk coverage for Hormuz passage. Iran&rsquo;s conditional September 22 offer &mdash; even if immediately accepted &mdash; would require a 7-day reopening sequence followed by the 7-day rolling average reaching 30 vessels before October 3; the remaining 10-day window makes this arithmetically infeasible. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>The Israeli election campaign continued against the backdrop of war and high inflation. Haaretz published a poll on September 23 showing Eisenkot&rsquo;s opposition bloc ahead of Netanyahu&rsquo;s coalition bloc in projected seat totals. Competing polls this week show Likud at 19 to 27 seats and Yashar at 22 to 25 seats depending on the survey, with high inter-poll variance; the plurality of current surveys gives Yashar the single-party seat lead, with i24NEWS/Direct as the sole outlier at Likud 27/Yashar 22. The Knesset remains dissolved since July 17 and the election date stands at October 27, leaving both F7 YES paths inoperable before election day. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span>, supports <span class="cite">F7</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 4. SCENARIO MAP -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
