      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (30%):</strong> F8 fails (52%) and the post-UNGA diplomatic vacuum allows resumed high-tempo US&ndash;Iran strikes, with widening risk to Saudi energy infrastructure and Gulf shipping beyond Hormuz.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (50%):</strong> F8 materializes (48%) but Iran&rsquo;s maximalist Hormuz conditions remain unmet; F9 resolves NO; Hormuz stays below 20 vessels/day through the Israeli election on October 27.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (20%):</strong> F8 leads to F9 (correlated); a dual-sided Hormuz corridor agreement takes shape before October 10, enabling insurer re-entry and the beginning of a broader ceasefire framework.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 5. INSIDE IRAN -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode &middot; 200 words &middot; verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media (IRNA, Press TV, IRIB) framed the September 22 UNGA exchange as a demonstration of diplomatic consistency on Iran&rsquo;s terms rather than a resumption of mutual negotiations. IRIB reported that Araghchi &ldquo;accepted&rdquo; Witkoff&rsquo;s request for a meeting &mdash; a framing that presents Iran as responding to American need rather than seeking engagement on its own initiative. Press TV carried Araghchi&rsquo;s presentation of Hormuz conditions as &ldquo;non-negotiable prerequisites,&rdquo; not an opening bid. No state outlet described the UNGA exchange as a reopening of formal negotiations or a change in Iran&rsquo;s stated conditions. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span> without move</p>

      <p>The seven-day Hormuz reopening offer was packaged in state media as a display of Iranian operational control over global energy markets: the message was that Tehran can reopen the strait quickly at its own discretion, once the US complies. Sputnik (Persian) quoted Pezeshkian at the SCO summit framing diplomacy as Iran&rsquo;s preferred path while attributing the current impasse entirely to US violations of the Islamabad MoU commitments. Tasnim News Agency ran commentary attributing Trump&rsquo;s &ldquo;annihilate or deal&rdquo; framing to domestic political pressure rather than genuine military intent. The domestic state-media narrative constructs Iran as a party that has never wavered on its core demands and is watching the US decide whether to comply. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span>, supports <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

